[[:db/cas 17592186163300 :tutorial/content "Let's once again consider the line integral with respect to arc length of $f(x,y)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s\n$$\n\nThere are two more important properties of this type of line integral that we need to be aware of.\n\n- There is usually more than one way to parametrize a curve. However, line integrals with respect to arc length are *independent of the choice of parametrization*. In other words, we get the same result regardless of which parametrization we choose.\n\nFor example, the paths $C_1$ and $C_2,$ given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\,{r}_{1}(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}t⟩, & 0\\le t<2\\pi  \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\,{r}_{2}(u) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}2u,\\,\\operatorname{sin}2u⟩, & 0\\le u<\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nboth traverse a circle of radius $1$ once in the counterclockwise direction starting from $(1,0).$ However, since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of parametrization, we have\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_2} f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s\n$$\n\n- Line integrals with respect to arc length are also *independent of the orientation* of $C.$ For example, suppose that $C$ is a path along a particular curve traversed from the point $A$ to the point $B.$ Then, $-C$ is a path along the same curve but traversed from $B$ to $A,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4f68e10429fa4bda69366751c303216d27df995edf2502b61ab9bfe84c3ff45.png)\n\nIndependence of orientation means that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{-C} f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\n- Finally, it's important to mention that all of the properties discussed in this lesson apply to line integrals with respect to arc length over space curves, i.e., line integrals of the form $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{C} f(x,y,z)\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate point 2 above, consider the line integral\n\\[\n\\int\\limits_C x\\,\\textrm d s, \n\\]\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $A(0,0)$ to $B(2,2).$ \n\nOne possible parameterization of $C$ from $A$ to $B$ is $\\mathbf r = \\langle t, t\\rangle$ for $t\\in[0,2].$ We get $x=y=t$ on the curve, and $\\mathbf r ' = \\langle 1, 1\\rangle$ which leads to $||\\mathbf r'|| = \\sqrt 2.$ Therefore, compuing the line integral gives\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\limits_C x\\,\\textrm d s = \\sqrt 2\\int_0^2 t\\,\\textrm{d}t = 2\\sqrt 2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nIf we *reverse* the direction of the curve, traversing from $B$ to $A,$ we get the parametrization \n\\[\n\\mathbf r(0+2-t) = \\mathbf r(2-t) =   \\langle 2-t,2- t\\rangle,\\quad t \\in[0,2].\n\\]\nNow, we get $x=y=2-t, \\mathbf r' = \\langle -1, -1\\rangle$ and so $||\\mathbf r'|| = \\sqrt 2.$ Computing the line integral using this parametrization gives\n\\[\n\\int\\limits_C x\\,\\textrm d s = \\sqrt 2\\int_0^2 (2-t)\\,\\textrm{d}t = 2\\sqrt 2.\n\\]" "Let's once again consider the line integral with respect to arc length of $f(x,y)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s\n$$\n\nThere are two more important properties of this type of line integral that we need to be aware of.\n\n- There is usually more than one way to parametrize a curve. However, line integrals with respect to arc length are *independent of the choice of parametrization*. In other words, we get the same result regardless of which parametrization we choose.\n\nFor example, the paths $C_1$ and $C_2,$ given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\,{\\mathbf{r}}_{1}(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}t⟩, & 0\\le t<2\\pi  \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\,{\\mathbf{r}}_{2}(u) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}2u,\\,\\operatorname{sin}2u⟩, & 0\\le u<\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nboth traverse a circle of radius $1$ once in the counterclockwise direction starting from $(1,0).$ However, since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of parametrization, we have\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_2} f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s\n$$\n\n- Line integrals with respect to arc length are also *independent of the orientation* of $C.$ For example, suppose that $C$ is a path along a particular curve traversed from the point $A$ to the point $B.$ Then, $-C$ is a path along the same curve but traversed from $B$ to $A,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4f68e10429fa4bda69366751c303216d27df995edf2502b61ab9bfe84c3ff45.png)\n\nIndependence of orientation means that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{-C} f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\n- Finally, it's important to mention that all of the properties discussed in this lesson apply to line integrals with respect to arc length over space curves, i.e., line integrals of the form $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{C} f(x,y,z)\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate point 2 above, consider the line integral\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C x\\,\\textrm d s,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $A(0,0)$ to $B(2,2).$ \n\nOne possible parameterization of $C$ from $A$ to $B$ is $\\mathbf r = \\langle t, t\\rangle$ for $t\\in[0,2].$ We get $x=y=t$ on the curve, and $\\mathbf r ' = \\langle 1, 1\\rangle$ which leads to $||\\mathbf r'|| = \\sqrt 2.$ Therefore, compuing the line integral gives\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int\\limits_C x\\,\\textrm d s &=\\sqrt 2\\int_0^2 t\\,\\textrm{d}t = 2\\sqrt 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nIf we *reverse* the direction of the curve, traversing from $B$ to $A,$ we get the parametrization \n$$\n\\mathbf r(0+2-t) = \\mathbf r(2-t) =   \\langle 2-t,2- t\\rangle,\\quad t \\in[0,2].\n$$\nNow, we get $x=y=2-t, \\mathbf r' = \\langle -1, -1\\rangle$ and so $||\\mathbf r'|| = \\sqrt 2.$ Computing the line integral using this parametrization gives\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C x\\,\\textrm d s = \\sqrt 2\\int_0^2 (2-t)\\,\\textrm{d}t = 2\\sqrt 2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186256721 :question/problem "Suppose $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=4\n$$ for some function $f(x,y),$ where ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{1}(t)=⟨{t}^{4}-3{t}^{2},\\,t⟩$ for $0\\le t\\le 2.$\n\nWhich of the following must be true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Suppose $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=4\n$$ for some function $f(x,y),$ where ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${\\mathbf{r}}_{1}(t)=⟨{t}^{4}-3{t}^{2},\\,t⟩$ for $0\\le t\\le 2.$\n\nWhich of the following must be true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186256722 :question/problem "Suppose $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=1\n$$ for some function $f(x,y),$ where ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by $$\n\\displaystyle {r}_{1}(u)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right)⟩\n$$ for $0\\le u\\le 2\\pi .$ \n\nWhich of the following must be true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Suppose $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=1\n$$ for some function $f(x,y),$ where ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by $$\n\\displaystyle {\\mathbf{r}}_{1}(u)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right)⟩\n$$ for $0\\le u\\le 2\\pi .$ \n\nWhich of the following must be true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186256723 :question/problem "Suppose $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=3\n$$ for some function $f(x,y),$ where ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{1}(t)=⟨t,\\,{t}^{3/2}⟩$ for $0\\le t\\le 4.$\n\nWhich of the following must be true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Suppose $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=3\n$$ for some function $f(x,y),$ where ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${\\mathbf{r}}_{1}(t)=⟨t,\\,{t}^{3/2}⟩$ for $0\\le t\\le 4.$\n\nWhich of the following must be true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186256734 :question/problem "Consider the curves ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2},$ defined parametrically as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\,{r}_{1}(t) & =⟨\\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+1},\\,3t⟩, & 0\\le t\\le \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\,{r}_{2}(u) & =⟨\\sqrt{4{u}^{2}+1},\\,2u⟩, & 0\\le u\\le \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow suppose $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k\n$$ for some function $f(x,y)$ and real number $k.$ Which of the following must be true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Consider the curves ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2},$ defined parametrically as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\,{\\mathbf{r}}_{1}(t) & =⟨\\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+1},\\,3t⟩, & 0\\le t\\le \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\,{\\mathbf{r}}_{2}(u) & =⟨\\sqrt{4{u}^{2}+1},\\,2u⟩, & 0\\le u\\le \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow suppose $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k\n$$ for some function $f(x,y)$ and real number $k.$ Which of the following must be true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186256735 :question/problem "Consider the curves ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2},$ defined parametrically as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\,{r}_{1}(t) & =⟨2t,\\,2\\sqrt{t}⟩, & 0\\le t\\le 2 \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\,{r}_{2}(u) & =⟨4u,\\,2\\sqrt{2u}⟩, & 0\\le u\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow suppose $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k\n$$ for some function $f(x,y)$ and real number $k.$ Which of the following must be true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Consider the curves ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2},$ defined parametrically as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\,{\\mathbf{r}}_{1}(t) & =⟨2t,\\,2\\sqrt{t}⟩, & 0\\le t\\le 2 \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\,{\\mathbf{r}}_{2}(u) & =⟨4u,\\,2\\sqrt{2u}⟩, & 0\\le u\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow suppose $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k\n$$ for some function $f(x,y)$ and real number $k.$ Which of the following must be true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186459283 :question/problem "Compute the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by the vectors $a=⟨4,0,2⟩$, $b=⟨1,3,0⟩$, and $c=⟨0,2,5⟩.$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" "Compute the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by the vectors $\\mathbf{a}=⟨4,0,2⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨1,3,0⟩$, and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨0,2,5⟩.$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186484224 :question/problem "Compute the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by the vectors $p=⟨1,2,3⟩$, $q=⟨0,5,1⟩$, and $r=⟨4,0,6⟩.$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" "Compute the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by the vectors $\\mathbf{p}=⟨1,2,3⟩$, $\\mathbf{q}=⟨0,5,1⟩$, and $\\mathbf{r}=⟨4,0,6⟩.$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186484229 :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $a=⟨2,k,-1⟩$, $b=⟨-3,4,5⟩$, and $c=⟨6,k,1⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" "Find $k$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{a}=⟨2,k,-1⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨-3,4,5⟩$, and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨6,k,1⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186484248 :question/problem "Calculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by $x=⟨3,0,-2⟩$, $y=⟨1,2,4⟩$, and $z=⟨-5,1,1⟩.$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" "Calculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\mathbf{x}=⟨3,0,-2⟩$, $\\mathbf{y}=⟨1,2,4⟩$, and $\\mathbf{z}=⟨-5,1,1⟩.$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186493949 :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $x=⟨1,k,2⟩$, $y=⟨5,-2,3⟩$, and $z=⟨-4,k,1⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" "Find $k$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{x}=⟨1,k,2⟩$, $\\mathbf{y}=⟨5,-2,3⟩$, and $\\mathbf{z}=⟨-4,k,1⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186544819 :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $a=⟨1,2,k⟩$, $b=⟨-3,0,4⟩$, and $c=⟨2,-1,5⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" "Find $k$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{a}=⟨1,2,k⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨-3,0,4⟩$, and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨2,-1,5⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186572689 :question/problem "Compute the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by the vectors $p=⟨1,0,5⟩$, $q=⟨3,2,6⟩$, and $r=⟨0,4,1⟩.$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" "Compute the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by the vectors $\\mathbf{p}=⟨1,0,5⟩$, $\\mathbf{q}=⟨3,2,6⟩$, and $\\mathbf{r}=⟨0,4,1⟩.$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186572715 :question/problem "Compute the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by the vectors $x=⟨2,3,0⟩$, $y=⟨1,4,5⟩$, and $z=⟨0,2,6⟩.$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" "Compute the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by the vectors $\\mathbf{x}=⟨2,3,0⟩$, $\\mathbf{y}=⟨1,4,5⟩$, and $\\mathbf{z}=⟨0,2,6⟩.$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186572726 :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $a=⟨k,k,2⟩$, $b=⟨1,3,0⟩$, and $c=⟨0,4,5⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" "Find $k$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{a}=⟨k,k,2⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨1,3,0⟩$, and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨0,4,5⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186622836 :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $p=⟨1,0,k⟩$, $q=⟨2,3,1⟩$, and $r=⟨0,4,5⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" "Find $k$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{p}=⟨1,0,k⟩$, $\\mathbf{q}=⟨2,3,1⟩$, and $\\mathbf{r}=⟨0,4,5⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186662822 :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $x=⟨1,3,2⟩$, $y=⟨0,-1,k⟩$, and $z=⟨2,5,1⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" "Find $k$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{x}=⟨1,3,2⟩$, $\\mathbf{y}=⟨0,-1,k⟩$, and $\\mathbf{z}=⟨2,5,1⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186572160 :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda (4a+\\sqrt{7}b)$ and $4\\sqrt{7}\\,a+7b$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $\\lambda .$\n\n$\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}" "Given that $\\lambda (4\\mathbf{a}+\\sqrt{7}\\mathbf{b})$ and $4\\sqrt{7}\\,\\mathbf{a}+7\\mathbf{b}$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $\\lambda .$\n\n$\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186180022 :question/problem "![](images/27/270e72ebedac3741e1b7086021b16dc0975ae9852d402673c8044a67f8f774fb.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and the vector field $F,$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the **clockwise** direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "![](images/27/270e72ebedac3741e1b7086021b16dc0975ae9852d402673c8044a67f8f774fb.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and the vector field $\\mathbf{F},$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the **clockwise** direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186180023 :question/problem "![](images/0a/0ae64bf57444ac42ec5efed392471cbbac7b1e317bc1fb06b33bcd2038a2e3c1.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and the vector field $F,$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "![](images/0a/0ae64bf57444ac42ec5efed392471cbbac7b1e317bc1fb06b33bcd2038a2e3c1.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and the vector field $\\mathbf{F},$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186180023 :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ are false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves counterclockwise along $C.$ \n\n![](images/bc/bc0121782b16d513d7d17d3741bd07ef1716e096bb2459fbda0cb841c7fd8b43.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves **clockwise** along $-C.$\n\n![](images/1a/1a6b109bc1da3aa7905b05f7f7902523b45fd51a65b97bec1328037bd6c0cfe7.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ only." "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}>0\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0\n$$ are false. It's clear from the picture below that $\\mathbf{F}$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves counterclockwise along $C.$ \n\n![](images/bc/bc0121782b16d513d7d17d3741bd07ef1716e096bb2459fbda0cb841c7fd8b43.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}>0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $\\mathbf{F}$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves **clockwise** along $-C.$\n\n![](images/1a/1a6b109bc1da3aa7905b05f7f7902523b45fd51a65b97bec1328037bd6c0cfe7.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}>0\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186180026 :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$∇f$ is conservative” is true. The vector field $∇f$ is conservative by definition.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” is true, while statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ for any piecewise smooth path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” is false. The result $Γ=0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$∇f$ is conservative”; “$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” only." "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$∇f$ is conservative” is true. The vector field $∇f$ is conservative by definition.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0\n$$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” is true, while statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0\n$$ for any piecewise smooth path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” is false. The result $Γ=0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$∇f$ is conservative”; “$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0\n$$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186180027 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=-\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i-\\frac{2y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j.\n$$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Consider the vector field $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf{F}(x,y)=-\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{2y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\mathbf{j}.\n$$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186180027 :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nWe notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{4xy}{(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$F$ is conservative” is true. The vector field $F$ is conservative since the partial derivatives are equal and continuous everywhere on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ for any piecewise smooth path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” is false, while statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” is true. The result $Γ=0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$F$ is conservative”; “$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” only." "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0.\n$$\n\nWe notice that $\\mathbf{F}=P\\,\\mathbf{i}+Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{4xy}{(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative” is true. The vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative since the partial derivatives are equal and continuous everywhere on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0\n$$ for any piecewise smooth path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” is false, while statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0\n$$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” is true. The result $Γ=0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative”; “$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0\n$$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186259803 :question/problem "![](images/dc/dca985dc352c45477dbb6ada86ad5a9cfa4404969510191a1955e59b082709fe.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "![](images/dc/dca985dc352c45477dbb6ada86ad5a9cfa4404969510191a1955e59b082709fe.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186259803 :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ is true. Since the force $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves around $C,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/eb/eb4da8143700c7d31e0ceacd09ac76a8d1bcb10379a34dce5b59d48f2ae58c13.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ remains unchanged.\n\n![](images/70/70a473397216589350f63482ab3e36cb1247e7825046c70642254c8514564b00.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ only." "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}>0\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0\n$$ is true. Since the force $\\mathbf{F}$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves around $C,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/eb/eb4da8143700c7d31e0ceacd09ac76a8d1bcb10379a34dce5b59d48f2ae58c13.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}>0\n$$ is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $\\mathbf{F}$ remains unchanged.\n\n![](images/70/70a473397216589350f63482ab3e36cb1247e7825046c70642254c8514564b00.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186259834 :question/problem "![](images/e6/e60f7c0adc3e2ec8daa3203564c31bf660e6eca7e6394955cc73133ac3673ed5.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and the vector field $F,$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "![](images/e6/e60f7c0adc3e2ec8daa3203564c31bf660e6eca7e6394955cc73133ac3673ed5.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and the vector field $\\mathbf{F},$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186259834 :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is true, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves counterclockwise along $C.$ \n\n![](images/a8/a8010db8481768cb61d0734dcfc97eed646441ab265c4928d06d274ef0e1c1dd.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves **clockwise** along $C.$\n\n![](images/be/be263d706bfc3ef205be37aaed85d4984d3739d53cdaadb8cba88e3d5196ce93.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ only." "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}>0\n$$ is true, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}<0\n$$ is false. It's clear from the picture below that $\\mathbf{F}$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves counterclockwise along $C.$ \n\n![](images/a8/a8010db8481768cb61d0734dcfc97eed646441ab265c4928d06d274ef0e1c1dd.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}<0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $\\mathbf{F}$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves **clockwise** along $C.$\n\n![](images/be/be263d706bfc3ef205be37aaed85d4984d3739d53cdaadb8cba88e3d5196ce93.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}>0;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}<0\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186259835 :question/problem "![](images/f4/f4042d4eb43310480f3c75673befbf58067a89704d20b9a63f0f6c7272179f3e.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and the vector field $F,$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "![](images/f4/f4042d4eb43310480f3c75673befbf58067a89704d20b9a63f0f6c7272179f3e.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and the vector field $\\mathbf{F},$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186259835 :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the